# 13. INSERT

Now we have a table.

But it's empty.

```text
students
────────────────────
id | name | age
────────────────────
(empty)
```

We need to add data.

That's what `INSERT` does.

---

# Basic INSERT

```sql
INSERT INTO students (name, age, email)
VALUES ('Janusha', 28, 'janusha@example.com');
```

Because `id` is automatically generated by `SERIAL`, we don't need to provide it.

---

## Insert Multiple Rows

```sql
INSERT INTO students (name, age, email)
VALUES
    ('Janusha', 28, 'janusha@example.com'),
    ('Amal', 25, 'amal@example.com'),
    ('Kasuni', 27, 'kasuni@example.com'),
    ('Nimal', 30, 'nimal@example.com');
```

Now:

```text
students
────────────────────────────────
id | name    | age | email
────────────────────────────────
1  | Janusha | 28  | ...
2  | Amal    | 25  | ...
3  | Kasuni  | 27  | ...
4  | Nimal   | 30  | ...
```

---

## Why specify column names?

Prefer:

```sql
INSERT INTO students (name, age, email)
VALUES (...);
```

instead of:

```sql
INSERT INTO students
VALUES (...);
```

Explicit columns make your query clearer and safer if the table structure changes.

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

> `INSERT` is used to add new records to a table. I normally specify the target columns explicitly and then provide the corresponding values.

