# 14. SELECT

Now we want to retrieve the data.

That's what `SELECT` does.

---

## Select Everything

```sql
SELECT *
FROM students;
```

Result:

```text
id | name    | age | email
---|---------|-----|-------------------
1  | Janusha | 28  | janusha@example.com
2  | Amal    | 25  | amal@example.com
3  | Kasuni  | 27  | kasuni@example.com
```

---

## Select Specific Columns

Instead of:

```sql
SELECT *
FROM students;
```

you can use:

```sql
SELECT name, age
FROM students;
```

Result:

```text
name    | age
--------|----
Janusha | 28
Amal    | 25
Kasuni  | 27
```

---

## Why avoid `SELECT *` in production?

Because you might only need a few columns.

For example:

```sql
SELECT name, email
FROM students;
```

is often better than:

```sql
SELECT *
FROM students;
```

when you only need name and email.

This can reduce unnecessary data transfer and make queries clearer.

---

## Column Alias

You can rename a column in the result:

```sql
SELECT
    name AS student_name,
    age AS student_age
FROM students;
```

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

> `SELECT` is used to retrieve data from a database. I can select all columns using `SELECT *` or specify only the columns I need, which is generally preferable in application code.

