# 25. JOINs

Now we reach one of the **most important SQL concepts for interviews**.

A JOIN allows you to retrieve related data from multiple tables.

Suppose:

### students

| id | name    |
| -: | ------- |
|  1 | Janusha |
|  2 | Amal    |
|  3 | Kasuni  |

### results

| id | student_id | score |
| -: | ---------: | ----: |
|  1 |          1 |    85 |
|  2 |          1 |    90 |
|  3 |          2 |    75 |

We want:

```text
Student Name | Score
Janusha      | 85
Janusha      | 90
Amal         | 75
```

We need a JOIN.

---

# INNER JOIN

```sql
SELECT
    students.name,
    results.score
FROM students
INNER JOIN results
    ON students.id = results.student_id;
```

The relationship is:

```text
students.id
     =
results.student_id
```

---

## INNER JOIN Meaning

Only records that match in **both tables** are returned.

```text
Students       Results

    A  ──────── A
    B  ──────── B
    C

Result:
A
B
```

---

# LEFT JOIN

A LEFT JOIN returns:

> All rows from the left table + matching rows from the right table.

```sql
SELECT
    students.name,
    results.score
FROM students
LEFT JOIN results
    ON students.id = results.student_id;
```

If Kasuni has no result:

```text
name    | score
--------|------
Janusha | 85
Janusha | 90
Amal    | 75
Kasuni  | NULL
```

This is extremely useful when you want **all students**, even those without results.

---

# RIGHT JOIN

RIGHT JOIN returns:

> All rows from the right table + matching rows from the left table.

It is less commonly used because many queries can be rewritten using a LEFT JOIN by changing table order.

---

# FULL OUTER JOIN

Returns:

> All matching and non-matching rows from both tables.

Conceptually:

```text
Table A
   +
Table B
   ↓
Everything
```

---

# JOIN Cheat Sheet

| JOIN            | Returns                   |
| --------------- | ------------------------- |
| INNER JOIN      | Matching rows from both   |
| LEFT JOIN       | All left + matching right |
| RIGHT JOIN      | All right + matching left |
| FULL OUTER JOIN | Everything from both      |

---

## 🎤 Interview Answer

> A JOIN combines related data from multiple tables using a relationship between columns, typically primary and foreign keys. Common joins include INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, and FULL OUTER JOIN.

